 that and when you see them  do that it's worth taking it with a  grain of salt because there may be  business motivations  that attribution as its called you know  Bloomberg has reported that you know  that within Yahoo you know the evidence  backing up that assertion wasn't  ironclad which is what we reported you  know blaming an attack on a nation-state  actor is a really serious thing and  you've got to have pretty good evidence  to make that that claim you know it  again that's that's kind of in dispute  with you know inside the company this  breach the much bigger breach the  billion accounts again working with the  researcher in Andrew Komarov you know  what we discovered was that site those  are cyber criminals those are hackers  who sell primarily to spammers you know  kind of mega spammers and the  significance of that is you know this  breach occurred in 2013  you know the spammers have had access to  a billion user accounts for over three  years which means they had been taking  over people's accounts to send out spam  because if your account is kind of fresh  and clean in the you know parlance of  hacking  you know Yahoo's not gonna block emails  you send so spammers love that and you  know that's a pretty serious issue  because people have made a lot of money  off of this data for a pretty long time  and it does throw into question you know  was a nation-state behind the other  attack or was it another criminal group  looking to spam next question we have is  you know what user information was  accessed with the recent breach  passwords accounts content etc so you've  got basically if you signed up for a  yahoo account it's basically everything  you gave them its user name its password  I should say Yahoo hashed the password  which means they encrypted it but the  problem with this big breach is they  encrypted it in an insecure way so you  can crack the password using like Amazon  servers like you just you basically a  masu per computer or a server you know  at these passwords and you can just  crack them as well as date of birth a  cell phone number  backup email addresses security  questions and answered security  questions and answers' unencrypted  that's everything I mean that  information again could be used to  compromise not just your yahoo account  but any other accounts where you use  let's call it the same security  questions so your bank accounts you know  things like that  and I'll tell you an interesting side  note  as well working with the security  researcher you know he's one of these  guys who you know in tracking cyber  criminals he kind of monitors and  infiltrates their private communication  channels so their emails their servers  things like that and he saw a  transaction happening live which we  reported and he saw this database being  sold this billion user database to three  separate individuals those individuals  are very interesting two of them he very  easily identified as these mega spammers  these are groups that you know send spam  out on the orders of millions or  billions of emails the third one though  was not a spammer the third one though  was very compelling because the third  one was a person who messaged the seller  and said I need to check if these ten  individuals are in this database and  they were Komarov wouldn't tell us  exactly who they were he's doing the  process of notifying these people but  they were top US government and business  officials mostly that's not a spammer  interested in that information that is  according to komarov's assessment likely  a foreign intelligence service you know  saying hey we'll buy this database  because we can infiltrate top US  corporate and and government officials  via this breach that's really  significant because that's powerful data  in their hands the next question is what  you know shouldn't they go back to the  drawing board and restructure their  encryption capabilities well these are  the questions that Verizon and Yahoo are  both looking at because Verizon at this  point is basically has basically had to  disclose we lost everything we lost in  terms of user information it's all gone  so can we protect it again in the future  sure they're going to change passwords  they're gonna do other things to protect  user information but from Verizon  standpoint the Yahoo user you know user  base is a key reason why they were  buying the company you know they want  all of the members of Yahoo's you know  fantasy sports leagues all the members  of Yahoo's email services you know and  if users start to leave Yahoo or abandon  their accounts because they feel like  look this company can't protect me  that's gonna be a big problem for you  know for Verizon the good news is that  if this deal goes through Verizon is  known as a very tech savvy organization  they have some very very good  cybersecurity people there so you can  take some measure of comfort in that  they would the accounts of  we be better protected under that  scenario but structuring this stuff and  restructuring you know these protections  is a very hard you know challenge  especially when you're talking about a  merger is Yahoo guaranteed next question  is is Yahoo guaranteeing that this will  not happen again  another good question because as these  these sequential data breaches showed  Yahoo doesn't have a very good sense of  you know what compromises have happened  on its network you know and the problem  with that is as we had explained to us  by a cybersecurity kind of incident  responder as they call it my question of  them was Yahoo's been working with some  top cybersecurity investigators for the  past three months  you know scouring its network for these  breaches why couldn't they have seen  this and the answer is you know even  when you hire these top firms you're  never going to get a complete picture of  what happened on your network especially  if it's several years ago and next  question is why did they wait so long to  disclose it the answer is they probably  didn't know and they probably didn't  know until this cyber security  researcher passed the information to US  law enforcement which then passed it to  Yahoo in November so you really have to  question the level of visibility that a  company has into its own security  operations when they don't know for  three years that they lost a billion  accounts and you know I wanted to wanted  to thank everybody for you know for  tuning in today we got some great  questions and I hope you learned a  little bit about the back story of how  this breach came to light this is  something that we're gonna be following  closely at Bloomberg you know as this  deal either progresses or it doesn't  these are high stakes for Yahoo and high  stakes for Verizon we really haven't  seen that before in terms of you know  data breach repercussions and as always  we'll be discussing it on the show  you know we want to take questions  offline as well so if you have any  further questions you want me to follow  up on a future show on Twitter I'm at  Jordan r1000 so please feel free to  message me there we'll try to get some  more questions next time and as always  we do this every week every Thursday at  1:00 p.m. Eastern thanks again for  everybody who wrote in  and look forward to talking to you all  next week
 

